WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
How can biologists test what drove evolution when history happened only once?

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:11.500
Experimental evolution watches replicate populations change under controlled

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.880
conditions. It also archives ancestors to compare past with present.

4
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.760
Split one genotype into replicate flasks differing in one factor.

5
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:32.500
In 1998, Rainey and Travisano grew the bacterium Pseudomonas fluorescens in unshaken

6
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:38.720
or shaken broth. Unshaken broth formed zones from the surface down, each a different

7
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:45.400
way to live. New heritable types evolved there; in shaken flasks that diversity did

8
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.400
not arise.

9
00:00:47.183 --> 00:00:51.543
As generations pass, biologists freeze samples to revive later.

10
00:00:53.023 --> 00:00:58.583
Replicate populations and archived ancestors turn evolutionary history into an

11
00:00:58.583 --> 00:01:00.023
experiment you can repeat.

12
00:01:01.263 --> 00:01:06.163
When bacteria evolve with the phages infecting them, samples are paired across time.

13
00:01:07.423 --> 00:01:09.183
That cross is a time-shift assay.

14
00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:14.127
Test phages from one week on past, same-time and future hosts.

15
00:01:15.387 --> 00:01:19.087
Without heritable change, every pairing gives similar infection.

16
00:01:20.347 --> 00:01:25.827
In an arms race, both sides escalate, so infection falls from past to future hosts.

17
00:01:27.107 --> 00:01:32.667
Under fluctuating selection, parasites track current hosts, so infection rises and

18
00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:33.667
falls in a wave.

19
00:01:34.617 --> 00:01:40.477
Now try one. A lab samples a bacterium and its phage for 20 weeks.

20
00:01:41.817 --> 00:01:46.757
In the time-shift assay, infection falls steadily from past hosts to future ones.

21
00:01:48.057 --> 00:01:50.037
Can the lab conclude this is an arms race?

22
00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:02.330
Not yet. A short record can cover one falling slope of a wave and look like an arms

23
00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:07.430
race. Long, dense records with replicate lines tell them apart.

24
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:13.090
Replicates also guard against drift, random change in small populations.

25
00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:18.110
Drift scatters lines, while selection pushes them the same way.

26
00:02:19.083 --> 00:02:22.683
Matched controls rule out shared lab or greenhouse effects.

27
00:02:23.923 --> 00:02:29.703
To separate coevolution from one-sided adaptation, Paterson and colleagues in 2010

28
00:02:29.703 --> 00:02:31.903
held the host at one genotype.

29
00:02:33.283 --> 00:02:37.063
Even then, coevolution needs heritable change in both partners.

30
00:02:38.303 --> 00:02:41.323
And flasks test mechanisms, not field communities.

31
00:02:42.533 --> 00:02:43.893
What has this unlocked?

32
00:02:45.173 --> 00:02:50.753
In 2008, Blount, Borland and Lenski grew twelve identical Escherichia coli

33
00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:56.413
populations. Their medium held glucose and citrate the cells could not use.

34
00:02:57.653 --> 00:03:03.153
One evolved citrate use in oxygen by about generation 31,500.

35
00:03:04.753 --> 00:03:10.173
Replaying frozen clones showed later generations evolved it more often, suggesting

36
00:03:10.173 --> 00:03:11.553
earlier changes mattered.

37
00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:14.707
Nature archives in pond mud.

38
00:03:16.007 --> 00:03:22.747
In 2007, Decaestecker and colleagues revived dormant Daphnia water-flea eggs and

39
00:03:22.747 --> 00:03:25.187
parasite spores from dated sediment layers.

40
00:03:26.567 --> 00:03:30.827
This resurrection ecology showed parasites adapted within a few years.

41
00:03:32.207 --> 00:03:35.367
Virulence, the harm to infected hosts, rose through time.

42
00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:43.190
In 2017, Gervasi and Schiestl pollinated replicate field mustard populations by

43
00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.930
bumblebees, by hoverflies, or by hand.

44
00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.110
Hand pollination was the control.

45
00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:55.650
Nine generations later, compared at generation eleven, bumblebee lines were taller

46
00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.470
and hoverfly lines self-pollinated more.

47
00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:04.250
Replicates, controls and archived ancestors test evolution directly.
